PORT=8080

# DATABASE_URL is supplied by root compose in full-stack dev (see
# ../deploy/docker-compose.dev.yml). It's the Postgres-backed job queue
# (ADR 003) — the Orchestrator claims jobs from the same `jobs` table the API
# writes to.
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql://yggdrasil:yggdrasil@localhost:5432/yggdrasil

# KUBECONFIG is a local-dev/test convenience only (internal/k8s/client.go's
# NewClient()) — NOT the real cluster-resolution path. Per ADR 016, every
# Organization configures its own target cluster via the web UI, and the
# Orchestrator resolves each job's cluster dynamically from that at claim
# time. Leave this unset unless you're running a tool that calls NewClient()
# directly — see docs/overview/setup.md.
# KUBECONFIG=

# Optional tuning (defaults shown):
# QUEUE_POLL_INTERVAL=2s
# WORKER_ID=
# JOB_RUNTIME_CLASS= (unset unless the target cluster has gVisor/Kata installed)
# At most this many jobs run concurrently **per replica** (ADR 006 item 1); the
# queue itself is safe with any number of replicas. Unset or invalid means the
# built-in default (worker.defaultMaxConcurrentJobs, currently 5) — note a
# non-positive value is ignored rather than taken as "run nothing".
# MAX_CONCURRENT_JOBS=5

# Per-job-kind agent-images images (ADR 004: yggdrasil-agent-images), built
# and pushed by that repo's CI to ghcr.io/yggdrasil-hq/yggdrasil-agent-images
# (not a per-install registry — see agent-images/docs/concepts/images.md). A
# kind left unset falls back to JOB_PLACEHOLDER_IMAGE/JOB_PLACEHOLDER_SCRIPT
# below — unset by default in local dev.
#
# GHCR packages are private on first push, and their visibility can differ per
# package: `test_run` currently refuses an anonymous pull while the others allow
# one. The Orchestrator asks the registry which is which and reports an
# unpullable image as a setup error rather than a timeout — see
# docs/overview/setup.md. If one of these needs a credential, create it in the
# target cluster and point JOB_IMAGE_PULL_SECRET at it (below).
SPEC_GRILL_IMAGE=ghcr.io/yggdrasil-hq/yggdrasil-agent-images/spec_grill:latest
FEATURE_BUILD_IMAGE=ghcr.io/yggdrasil-hq/yggdrasil-agent-images/feature_build:latest
TEST_RUN_IMAGE=ghcr.io/yggdrasil-hq/yggdrasil-agent-images/test_run:latest
DESIGN_GRILL_IMAGE=ghcr.io/yggdrasil-hq/yggdrasil-agent-images/design_grill:latest
# The other two agent-driven kinds (ADR 015). `script_test_run` is set here so a
# default install is complete: the API dispatches a unit and an integration probe
# for every feature, unconditionally (script presence in the project's repo is
# the group's toggle, ADR 015 item 10), so leaving it unset makes every feature
# report two groups that could not run. Its package is publicly pullable, so
# setting it needs no credential.
SCRIPT_TEST_RUN_IMAGE=ghcr.io/yggdrasil-hq/yggdrasil-agent-images/script_test_run:latest
# AGENTIC_REVIEW_IMAGE=ghcr.io/yggdrasil-hq/yggdrasil-agent-images/agentic_review:latest

# Name of a docker-config object in each project's namespace that job pods
# present when pulling the images above. Required only for images whose package
# is not publicly pullable; the Orchestrator reports which ones those are.
#
# A namespaced credential is not used merely by existing — a pod or its service
# account has to reference it — and the Orchestrator sets no ServiceAccountName,
# so without this (or a reference on the namespace's `default` service account)
# the credential is inert. See docs/overview/setup.md.
# JOB_IMAGE_PULL_SECRET=ghcr-pull

# Placeholder dev stand-in, used for any job kind not covered above:
# JOB_PLACEHOLDER_IMAGE=busybox:1.36
# JOB_PLACEHOLDER_SCRIPT=echo job $JOB_ID kind=$JOB_KIND; exit 0

# Required for `deploy` jobs (ADR 003 §16) and for every other job kind's
# per-project model config (ADR 004: MODEL_BASE_URL/MODEL_API_KEY/MODEL_ID) —
# where to fetch decrypted project secrets from, and the shared bearer token
# to authenticate with. The same INTERNAL_API_TOKEN value must be set in
# ../api/.env.
API_INTERNAL_URL=http://api:3000
INTERNAL_API_TOKEN=dev-internal-api-token-change-me

# Primary deployment ingress (ADR 003 §15) — defaults match a local k3s
# install (docs/overview/setup.md): Traefik (k3s's bundled ingress controller)
# and a selfSigned cert-manager ClusterIssuer. A self-hosted/managed install
# pointing at a real domain swaps these for ingress-nginx + a real ACME
# ClusterIssuer — config only, no code change.
#
# For local dev, set APPS_BASE_DOMAIN to a wildcard-to-localhost DNS name
# (nip.io resolves <anything>.127.0.0.1.nip.io to 127.0.0.1, no /etc/hosts
# editing needed) instead of the "yggdrasil.local" placeholder below, which
# doesn't resolve anywhere. Must match api/.env's APPS_BASE_DOMAIN exactly
# (the two are set independently — see docs/conventions/deploy.md).
# APPS_BASE_DOMAIN=127.0.0.1.nip.io
# INGRESS_CLASS_NAME=traefik
# CERT_ISSUER_NAME=selfsigned-issuer

# --- Ephemeral preview deployments (ADR 003 §10/§15/§17) ---
# Per-project cap on simultaneously-live previews. Admission is enforced when a
# job is *claimed*, so an over-cap preview-eligible job stays queued rather than
# failing. Note this variable's scale is its own: 0 (and unset) mean the default
# of 3, NOT "off" — **a negative value is what disables previews**, which also
# removes the previews table from the claim query. That is the supported way to
# run against a database whose migrations predate 038_job_previews.sql.
# MAX_CONCURRENT_PREVIEWS=3
# How long a preview may outlive its job when nothing else knows the job is gone
# (a hard-crashed job stays `running` forever, so job status alone can never
# collect its preview).
# PREVIEW_TTL=2h
# How often the orphan sweep runs — startup plus this interval.
# PREVIEW_SWEEP_INTERVAL=15m

# --- Preview image builds (ADR 003 §12/§14, issue #19) ---
# The registry a preview's image is built and pushed to, and from which the
# preview's Deployment then pulls. **Unset (the default) disables the build
# entirely**: there would be nowhere to push and nowhere to pull, so a preview
# keeps the image its chart declares (the scaffolded template's
# `nginxdemos/hello`) exactly as it did before this existed, and not even the API
# is asked. A malformed hostname is therefore harmless — it is not used unless
# set.
#
# Set it to the bundled in-cluster registry of ADR 003 §14 (e.g.
# `registry.registry.svc.cluster.local:5000`) or to a reachable external one.
# The build pushes to `<registry>/proj-<project-id>/<repo>:<sanitised-ref>`, so
# the registry must accept that path — and the cluster must be able to pull what
# it pushed, which is what the auth secret below is for.
# IMAGE_REGISTRY=registry.registry.svc.cluster.local:5000
# Names a docker-config Secret in the *project's* namespace carrying push
# credentials for IMAGE_REGISTRY, mounted at Kaniko's `/kaniko/.docker`. Unset is
# correct for the bundled `registry:2`, which takes unauthenticated pushes inside
# the cluster. It must already exist in each project namespace — this does not
# create it, and a missing Secret fails the build (which degrades to the chart's
# image rather than failing the job).
# IMAGE_BUILD_AUTH_SECRET=registry-push

# --- Screen recordings (ADR 029) ---
# Cap on the recording this replica will collect from a finished test_run pod.
# Unlike MAX_CONCURRENT_PREVIEWS, a non-positive value here **does** disable
# collection — the supported way to run against a database whose migrations
# predate 041_job_recordings.sql — while an unparseable value falls back to the
# default rather than to "off", so a typo cannot silently drop every artifact.
# RECORDING_MAX_BYTES=25000000

# --- Step screenshots (issue #22) ---
# Cap on *each* screenshot this replica will collect from a finished job pod and
# upload. Per-screenshot, not per-job: a run has one screenshot per reported step.
#
# The same unset/non-positive/unparseable rules as RECORDING_MAX_BYTES above, and
# for the same reasons: unset takes the default, a non-positive value disables
# screenshot collection entirely, and a typo falls back to the default rather than
# silently dropping every artifact.
#
# Should agree with the API's SCREENSHOT_MAX_BYTES (2 MB by default), which is the
# authoritative cap. Set SCREENSHOT_MAX_BYTES=0 on both sides to keep the feature
# off. The API also enforces a per-job count (SCREENSHOT_MAX_PER_JOB, 50 by
# default) which is deliberately not mirrored here — see internal/worker/
# screenshots.go for why a second, silently divergent bound would be worse than a
# logged decline.
# SCREENSHOT_MAX_BYTES=2000000

# --- Grill reply bound (issue #82) ---
# How long one `ask_user` question may go unanswered before the run fails (rather
# than holding a pod, and the project's GitHub token and model key with it,
# indefinitely).
#
# Deliberately generous, because the grill is human-gated *by design*: 24h spans a
# working day plus an overnight gap, and the bound exists to catch "nobody is
# coming", not "the human is slow". A bound that fires on a slow but real human is
# worse than the stall it prevents — it discards a run that was going to finish.
#
# Like MAX_CONCURRENT_PREVIEWS, an unset or unparseable value takes the default
# rather than disabling anything: there is no useful configuration in which this
# process waits forever for a human, which is the bug #82 fixed, so a typo must not
# remove the bound. Use Go duration syntax (`24h`, `90m`) — the same grammar the API
# parses, so one value pastes into both files.
#
# **Set this on both sides, or the countdown lies.** The API mirrors this value to
# render a "waiting 18h" countdown on the Feature page (issue #92), and the two
# services share no `.env` — so raising it here alone leaves the API still
# reporting the 24h default. The API cannot detect that, because it cannot read this
# process's environment; it reports `timeoutSource: "default"` when it is using its
# own copy, which is the signal a client uses to hedge the countdown. Both defaults
# are pinned by tests on each side (#96), so a change to either is a visible
# decision rather than a silent drift.
# GRILL_REPLY_TIMEOUT=24h
